From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eryk Wieliczko <eryk@wieliczko.ninja>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:55:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2764: Keep the fault IRQ masked across system sleep

From the component suspend until its resume, the fault handler can
neither read nor clear the interrupt latches: the regmap is cache-only,
and the I2C controller suspends after the card. A fault latched in that
window, such as the TDM clock fault when the bus clock stops, holds the
shared level-low line asserted. Every amp's handler then fails to read
its latches and returns IRQ_NONE, in a loop until the IRQs are
suspended, and again from resume_device_irqs() until the deferred
component resume. On a J713 one suspend logged "tas2764_irq_locked:
15208 callbacks suppressed" and "interrupt latch N unreadable". Enough
of it can make the spurious-IRQ detector disable the line, and with it
the SDZ group's fault shutdown.

Disable the IRQ at the start of the component suspend and enable it
again at the end of the resume, on every path. disable_irq() nests
across the amps sharing the line, and ASoC resumes every component it
suspended, even after a failed suspend. A clock fault latched in sleep
is then handled after the resume, as before.

Signed-off-by: Eryk Wieliczko <eryk@wieliczko.ninja>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 9abd087aaa6136108e365e05a172c27ab2919d08)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
index 8de574533e..a5c2c7c462 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
@@ -334,6 +334,18 @@ static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * Until resume the handler can neither read nor clear the latches: the
+	 * regmap goes cache-only below, and the I2C controller suspends after
+	 * the card. A fault latched meanwhile, such as the clock fault when the
+	 * bus clock stops, would hold the shared level-low line asserted and
+	 * run every amp's handler in a loop. Keep the line masked until the
+	 * cache is live again; disable_irq() nests across the amps sharing it.
+	 * ASoC resumes every suspended component, even after a failed suspend.
+	 */
+	if (tas2764->irq_requested)
+		disable_irq(tas2764->irq);
+
 	tas2764_flush_shutdown(tas2764);
 
 	ret = snd_soc_component_update_bits(component, TAS2764_PWR_CTRL,
@@ -368,7 +380,7 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 		ret = tas2764->sdz_reg ? regulator_enable(tas2764->sdz_reg) : 0;
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(tas2764->dev, "Failed to enable regulator\n");
-			return ret;
+			goto out_irq;
 		}
 
 		if (tas2764->sdz_gpio)
@@ -380,10 +392,14 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, false);
 
 	ret = regcache_sync(tas2764->regmap);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
+	if (ret >= 0)
+		ret = tas2764_update_pwr_ctrl(tas2764);
 
-	return tas2764_update_pwr_ctrl(tas2764);
+out_irq:
+	/* Balance the suspend; a fault latched in sleep is handled now. */
+	if (tas2764->irq_requested)
+		enable_irq(tas2764->irq);
+	return ret;
 }
 #else
 #define tas2764_codec_suspend NULL
